  <div class="doc">
    <header class="doc-head">
      <img src="{{ base }}/assets/brand/jcvb-logo-hz-on-light.png" alt="John Carroll Volleyball">
      <h1>Net System Research — 2026</h1>
      <p class="doc-sub">Prepared for JC Athletics administration &middot; upper &amp; lower gym volleyball net systems</p>
      <p class="doc-meta">Internal working document &mdash; not linked from the public site.</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lede">
      Our current net systems are past due for replacement. Below is a summary of the
      specific problems we're running into, what's driving them, and the range of
      replacement options &mdash; from a quick patch to a full no-anchor system with a
      built-in referee stand &mdash; with rough cost and quality tiers for each.
    </p>

    <h2>The problems</h2>
    <ol class="problem-list">
      <li>
        <span class="num">1</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Can't reliably get a net to proper height and full tautness.</strong>
          <span class="detail">Poles wobble inward toward the middle of the court under
          tension, and depending on which of our three court configurations we're using,
          we can't crank the net tight enough to get the top cable fully taut &mdash;
          it sags no matter how far we take the tensioner.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="num">2</span>
        <div>
          <strong>No reliable, complete set of poles and nets we can set up cleanly.</strong>
          <span class="detail">Years of setup/teardown across three different
          configurations (full-gym and two perpendicular half-gym courts), with
          individual pieces broken, mangled, or missing along the way, mean we can't
          count on a full matched set of poles and nets going up without hunting for
          or making do with something that's damaged.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="num">3</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Antennas keep breaking or don't fit.</strong>
          <span class="detail">Antennas fall off mid-play or won't stay clipped, and
          some no longer match the net we're pairing them with.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="num">4</span>
        <div>
          <strong>The nets themselves are torn along the sides.</strong>
          <span class="detail">Side damage means the net can't be pulled and anchored
          properly, which compounds the tautness problem above &mdash; a perfect pole
          and tensioner still can't square up a net that's failing at the edge.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout">
      <h3>Both gyms run three configurations &mdash; upper gym uses all three more often</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Both the upper and lower gym run <strong>three net configurations</strong>
        &mdash; full-gym and two perpendicular half-gym courts &mdash; each with its
        own base spacing (the exact spacing varies slightly between the two gyms).
        The two half-gym courts need to run <strong>at the same time, not one after
        the other</strong> &mdash; that's how we get JV and Varsity, boys and girls,
        practicing simultaneously in one gym. So the horizontal configuration isn't
        one net moved between two spots; it's two complete, working nets up at once.
        That's three times the setup/teardown wear on the same pole set in either
        gym, and it means whatever we buy needs poles and a tensioner rated for the
        narrowest of the three spans, not just the standard full-court width, plus
        enough complete sets to run two nets side by side. The upper gym uses all
        three configurations more often, which is what makes it the harder problem
        and the stronger case for the primary system. Any vendor quote should
        confirm compatibility with all three configurations explicitly, not just
        regulation full-court.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Patchwork options &mdash; getting through this season</h2>
    <p>Low/no-cost stopgaps for this season while a purchase works through budget approval.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Patch</th>
          <th>What it does</th>
          <th class="cost">Who / rough cost</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Facilities re-tightens/resets the sleeve collars &amp; anchor hardware</td>
          <td>Wobble may be a loose floor sleeve, not the pole &mdash; worth checking
            before assuming we need new hardware.</td>
          <td class="cost">Facilities &mdash; labor only</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Shim the pole-to-sleeve fit</td>
          <td>Pipe-wrap tape or a rubber shim kit on the pole base so it seats
            snugly instead of rocking in a worn sleeve.</td>
          <td class="cost">Facilities &mdash; &lt;$30/pole</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Raise/pack the bottom of the sleeve</td>
          <td>A spacer block seats the pole higher, taking up vertical play in a
            deep or worn sleeve.</td>
          <td class="cost">Facilities &mdash; scrap wood/rubber, ~$0</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Consolidate the best parts into one complete set</td>
          <td>Pool the working pieces across all three configurations into one
            matched set for the full-gym game configuration.</td>
          <td class="cost">Coaching staff/equip. mgr &mdash; time only</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

