    <h3>On the freestanding / no-anchor option</h3>
    <p>
      A few other schools have already moved to this style of system &mdash; a
      self-contained, heavily ballasted base (up to ~1,100&nbsp;lb per stand in the
      top-tier Sports Imports build) that gets bow-string-tight tension with no floor
      sleeves, anchors, or guy wires. Several versions bundle a referee stand directly
      into the base. It's the cleanest fix for the wobble problem specifically, since
      there's no worn sleeve in the mix at all, and the units reposition freely for
      any configuration without needing separate sleeve installs at each spot.
      One caveat on quantity: since both half-gym courts run at the same time, not
      one after the other, we'd need two full setups on hand &mdash; not one unit
      moved back and forth &mdash; to actually run the horizontal configuration.
      The tradeoff is storage: it stores on a cart/dolly when not in use, and needs a
      real footprint somewhere near the upper gym &mdash; worth confirming we have that
      space before going further down this path.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Sourcing input from other schools.</strong> Worth checking in with
      schools already on this type of system &mdash; which vendor, what they actually
      paid, how/where they store it, how it handles multiple court configurations,
      and any complaints on stability or the built-in ref stand.</p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Worth a direct look too: the <strong>McFaul Center</strong> (Bel Air Rec) runs a
      system that's held up well &mdash; durable and easy to set up/maintain, by
      reputation. Don't know the brand offhand;
      <a href="mailto:trina@belairrec.org">Trina at Bel Air Rec</a>
      (<a href="mailto:trina@belairrec.org">trina@belairrec.org</a>) would likely know
      the make/model and could speak to how it's held up.
    </p>

    <h2>Suggested approach</h2>
    <div class="rec-grid">
      <div class="rec-card">
        <p class="loc">Upper gym &mdash; primary system</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0">
          This is where games are played and where all three configurations get used,
          so it should get the better system. Either a heavier-duty sleeve-mounted
          competition package (if the existing floor sleeves check out sound), or the
          freestanding no-anchor system if we want to eliminate the sleeve/wobble
          question entirely and simplify going between the three court layouts.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rec-card">
        <p class="loc">Lower gym &mdash; second priority</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0">
          The reported wobble here specifically points at the floor sleeves/anchors,
          not just the poles. A mid-tier sleeve-mounted package is the budget-friendly
          fix if facilities confirms the sleeves can be tightened or reset; otherwise
          this is the other strong candidate for a freestanding unit, since it
          sidesteps the sleeve problem outright.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

